#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217980} :activity/id #uuid "f67186a8-3fe3-5bdd-b09e-4edfe15e8cef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049837 :topic/id #uuid "17cac727-fab1-5837-8069-a3e9364662a5" :topic/math-academy-id 6341} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218320 :tutorial/content "Consider the line\n\n$$\ny = x + 2\n$$\n\nand the parabola\n\n$$\ny = x^2 + x + 1\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/9d/9d7ca10163c9b5096f90e19bdb50d98881bd55e92cbaea4aa5af6696e728f9f3.png)\n\nWe can see from the diagram that there are two points of intersection. So let's calculate the coordinates of these intersection points.\n\nAn intersection point is a common solution of the equations\n\n$$\ny = x + 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = x^2 + x + 1\n$$\n\nIn other words, to get the coordinates of the intersection points, we have to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = x^{2} + x + 1; y = x + 2\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and then solving for $x$ using our knowledge of quadratic equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + x + 1 &= x + 2 \\\\\nx^{2} + x + 1 &= x + 2 \\\\\nx^{2} + 1 &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -1, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=1$.\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n$y|= x + 2; = (-1) + 2; = 1$,\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,1)$.\n- Substituting $x=1$, we get\n$y|= x + 2; = (1) + 2; = 3$,\nso the other point of intersection is $(1,3)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(-1,\\,1 \\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\,3 \\right)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b907a9fb-d730-5d33-b47d-e448e4bc64dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13862 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a89077dd-e912-5270-b496-6fe6281d817b" :step/math-academy-id 509403 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217108 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216959 :question/id #uuid "44ed3aff-8a4b-520c-808f-9e3ff928ac74" :question/math-academy-id "e-19814"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31b2bec9-3dff-557c-8cf5-a54f145fc45c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050984}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217334}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"} :step/id #uuid "1e5979d0-7029-563e-b231-e335fadaa16d" :step/math-academy-id 509404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217982}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216960 :question/id #uuid "9aa14c81-aeaa-51b7-9d16-468e22bc113d" :question/math-academy-id "e-19815"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217335}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"} :step/id #uuid "67ccd293-c59d-59e8-a1d5-686a88bbfc0e" :step/math-academy-id 509408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218321 :tutorial/content "![](images/b9/b9cb25bbb7b3076a3dae8688a232d18ec4092d68d9e815dedb438bc6900d97bb.png)\n\nA line and a parabola may have **two**,\n**one**, or **no** points of intersection.\n\nThis is similar to how a quadratic equation may have **two**,\n**one**, or **no** solutions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "befa8bb3-607a-57e3-b906-28765ffb7779" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13863 :tutorial/title "The Number of Intersection Points"} :step/id #uuid "5955a5e4-10f4-59b2-8eb7-8c63323e0526" :step/math-academy-id 509412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217984}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217110 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216961 :question/id #uuid "b24d4adc-8623-5fa9-9ac3-f95d7b4c0d1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-19816"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cf321a6-6ef6-58a2-ab52-176a923fc086" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217339}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Parabola and a Line"} :step/id #uuid "d1bb51f4-11dc-57e8-988f-57c86f8a0159" :step/math-academy-id 509413 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217111 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216962 :question/id #uuid "b2ead57b-8fd7-5d98-93fe-0e06290a5aa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-19817"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76453c35-6f8e-5564-80f7-33fc0c8edbde" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050503}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217336}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of an Unknown Parameter Given That There Are No Intersection Points"} :step/id #uuid "3812edf3-d3fb-5828-8887-e0cd46b17f0a" :step/math-academy-id 509417}] :activity/title "Finding Intersections of Lines and Quadratic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186217314}]}
